Chapter: C64Wasm
  cites C64 chapter Memory
  cites C64 chapter Cpu6502
  cites C64 chapter Cia
  cites C64 chapter RomData

 The browser interface for the emulator. Three facts about the machine
 decide the whole shape of this chapter.

 The C64's state already lives at fixed absolute addresses -- RAM at
 #A00000, the ROMs, colour RAM, the SID, VIC and CIA registers, all
 inside one band under #A18000. A wasm module's linear memory persists
 between calls and the emitter declares 256 pages (16 MB), so that band
 survives from one call to the next with no handle and no page-held
 state. This is a third state contract beside the two in
 games-backlog.md GAME-11: the machine IS the state, and it is already
 where it needs to be.

 What does NOT survive is the framebuffer. Bare metal renders to the GOP
 at #BF000000, which is 3.2 GB: past the declared memory, and past what
 an i32 store address can even name. The browser gets its own
 framebuffer low in the same linear memory, and the page reads the
 pixels straight out of it.

 The third fact is the cost of a CPU step. `cpu-step` answers a fresh
 CpuState, and a frame is 19,656 of them, so a frame allocates a couple
 of megabytes that no collector will ever take back. `c64w-frame` saves
 the heap pointer on the way in and restores it on the way out, and the
 registers cross that boundary through the fixed band rather than on the
 heap. Without it the bump allocator walks up into the machine's own
 memory within a few seconds of play.

Section: The Browser Machine's Addresses

 The framebuffer is 640 by 480 by 4 bytes = 1,228,800, so it runs
 #C00000 to #D2C000 and stays clear of both the machine band below it
 and the 16 MB ceiling above.

  c64w-fb : Integer = #C00000
  c64w-width : Integer = 640
  c64w-height : Integer = 480

 Fourteen registers, one 32-bit slot each, above the VIC registers at
 #A17000 and below the keyboard ring.

  c64w-cpu : Integer = #A19000

Section: Carrying the Registers Across a Call

  c64w-cpu-save : CpuState -> Integer
  c64w-cpu-save (s) =
    let d0 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 0 s.cpu-a
    in let d1 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 4 s.cpu-x
    in let d2 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 8 s.cpu-y
    in let d3 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 12 s.cpu-sp
    in let d4 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 16 s.cpu-pc
    in let d5 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 20 s.cpu-flag-c
    in let d6 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 24 s.cpu-flag-z
    in let d7 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 28 s.cpu-flag-i
    in let d8 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 32 s.cpu-flag-d
    in let d9 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 36 s.cpu-flag-b
    in let d10 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 40 s.cpu-flag-v
    in let d11 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 44 s.cpu-flag-n
    in let d12 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 48 s.cpu-cycles
    in let d13 = poke-32 c64w-cpu 52 s.cpu-halted
    in d0 + d1 + d2 + d3 + d4 + d5 + d6 + d7 + d8 + d9 + d10 + d11 + d12 + d13

  c64w-cpu-load : Integer -> CpuState
  c64w-cpu-load (dummy) =
    CpuState {
      cpu-a = peek-32 c64w-cpu 0,
      cpu-x = peek-32 c64w-cpu 4,
      cpu-y = peek-32 c64w-cpu 8,
      cpu-sp = peek-32 c64w-cpu 12,
      cpu-pc = peek-32 c64w-cpu 16,
      cpu-flag-c = peek-32 c64w-cpu 20,
      cpu-flag-z = peek-32 c64w-cpu 24,
      cpu-flag-i = peek-32 c64w-cpu 28,
      cpu-flag-d = peek-32 c64w-cpu 32,
      cpu-flag-b = peek-32 c64w-cpu 36,
      cpu-flag-v = peek-32 c64w-cpu 40,
      cpu-flag-n = peek-32 c64w-cpu 44,
      cpu-cycles = peek-32 c64w-cpu 48,
      cpu-halted = peek-32 c64w-cpu 52
    }

Section: The Palette

 The sixteen C64 colours, stored the way a canvas ImageData wants them.
 A little-endian 32-bit store lays the bytes down R, G, B, A, so each
 constant here is the bare-metal ARGB value of the same colour with red
 and blue exchanged. Writing them out once is cheaper than swapping two
 bytes on every one of the quarter-million pixels a frame stores.

  c64w-palette : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-palette (idx) =
    when bit-and idx 15
      is 0 -> #FF000000
      is 1 -> #FFFFFFFF
      is 2 -> #FF2B3768
      is 3 -> #FFB2A470
      is 4 -> #FF863D6F
      is 5 -> #FF438D58
      is 6 -> #FF792835
      is 7 -> #FF6FC7B8
      is 8 -> #FFB55E6C
      is 9 -> #FF294444
      is 10 -> #FF6E73A9
      is 11 -> #FF444444
      is 12 -> #FF6C6C6C
      is 13 -> #FF84D29A
      is 14 -> #FFB55E6C
      is 15 -> #FF959595
      is otherwise -> #FF000000

Section: Rendering

 Forty by twenty-five characters at 2x is 640 by 400, laid into a 640 by
 480 frame at y = 40, which leaves a forty-row border band above and
 below and none at the sides. Only those two bands need the border
 colour: the characters cover every pixel between them, so filling the
 whole frame first would store 307,200 pixels to overwrite 256,000 of
 them.

  c64w-fill-row : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  c64w-fill-row (y) (color) (x) =
    if x >= c64w-width then 0
    else let d = poke-32 c64w-fb ((y * c64w-width + x) * 4) color
    in d + c64w-fill-row y color (x + 1)

  c64w-fill-band : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  c64w-fill-band (y) (limit) (color) =
    if y >= limit then 0
    else let d = c64w-fill-row y color 0
    in d + c64w-fill-band (y + 1) limit color

  c64w-glyph-bits : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  c64w-glyph-bits (px) (y) (bits) (fg) (bg) (b) =
    if b >= 8 then 0
    else let color = if bit-and bits (bit-shl 1 (7 - b)) /= 0 then fg else bg
    in let x = px + b * 2
    in let off0 = (y * c64w-width + x) * 4
    in let off1 = ((y + 1) * c64w-width + x) * 4
    in let d0 = poke-32 c64w-fb off0 color
    in let d1 = poke-32 c64w-fb (off0 + 4) color
    in let d2 = poke-32 c64w-fb off1 color
    in let d3 = poke-32 c64w-fb (off1 + 4) color
    in d0 + d1 + d2 + d3 + c64w-glyph-bits px y bits fg bg (b + 1)

  c64w-glyph-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  c64w-glyph-rows (ch) (px) (py) (fg) (bg) (r) =
    if r >= 8 then 0
    else let bits = peek-byte char-rom (ch * 8 + r)
    in let y = py + r * 2
    in let d = c64w-glyph-bits px y bits fg bg 0
    in d + c64w-glyph-rows ch px py fg bg (r + 1)

  c64w-render-chars : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  c64w-render-chars (bg) (idx) =
    if idx >= 1000 then 0
    else let col = int-mod idx 40
    in let row = idx / 40
    in let ch = peek-byte c64-ram (#0400 + idx)
    in let fg = c64w-palette (bit-and (peek-byte color-ram-base idx) 15)
    in let d = c64w-glyph-rows ch (col * 16) (row * 16 + 40) fg bg 0
    in d + c64w-render-chars bg (idx + 1)

  c64w-render : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-render (dummy) =
    let bg = c64w-palette (peek-byte vic-state #21)
    in let border = c64w-palette (peek-byte vic-state #20)
    in let d0 = c64w-fill-band 0 40 border
    in let d1 = c64w-fill-band 440 c64w-height border
    in d0 + d1 + c64w-render-chars bg 0

Section: Running the Machine

  c64w-run-cycles : CpuState, Integer -> CpuState
  c64w-run-cycles (s) (remaining) =
    if remaining <= 0 then s
    else if s.cpu-halted /= 0 then s
    else c64w-run-cycles (cpu-step s) (remaining - 1)

 PAL: 312 raster lines of 63 cycles. Line number is published to the VIC
 raster register at #12 with its ninth bit in #11, because a program that
 waits on the raster is waiting on those two.

  c64w-run-raster : CpuState, Integer -> CpuState
  c64w-run-raster (s) (line) =
    if line >= 312 then s
    else let d = poke-byte vic-state #12 (bit-and line #FF)
    in let d2 = if line > 255
      then poke-byte vic-state #11 (bit-or (peek-byte vic-state #11) #80)
      else poke-byte vic-state #11 (bit-and (peek-byte vic-state #11) #7F)
    in c64w-run-raster (c64w-run-cycles s 63) (line + 1)

Section: Bringing the Machine Up

  c64w-init-screen : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-init-screen (i) =
    if i >= 1000 then 0
    else let d = poke-byte c64-ram (#0400 + i) 32
    in d + c64w-init-screen (i + 1)

  c64w-init-color : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-init-color (i) =
    if i >= 1000 then 0
    else let d = poke-byte color-ram-base i 14
    in d + c64w-init-color (i + 1)

 #2F and #37 are the 6510 data-direction register and processor port at
 $0000 and $0001: all ROMs and I/O banked in, which is what the KERNAL
 reset expects to find.

  c64w-init-ram : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-init-ram (dummy) =
    let d0 = poke-byte c64-ram 0 #2F
    in let d1 = poke-byte c64-ram 1 #37
    in d0 + d1 + c64w-init-screen 0 + c64w-init-color 0

 VIC control and memory pointers: 25 rows and the screen on, 40 columns,
 screen at $0400 with the character generator at $1000, no interrupts,
 light blue on blue.

  c64w-init-vic : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-init-vic (dummy) =
    let d0 = poke-byte vic-state #11 #1B
    in let d1 = poke-byte vic-state #16 #C8
    in let d2 = poke-byte vic-state #18 #15
    in let d3 = poke-byte vic-state #19 0
    in let d4 = poke-byte vic-state #20 14
    in let d5 = poke-byte vic-state #21 6
    in d0 + d1 + d2 + d3 + d4 + d5

Section: The Browser Interface

  c64w-reset : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-reset (dummy) =
    let d0 = c64w-init-ram 0
    in let d1 = cia-init 0
    in let d2 = c64w-init-vic 0
    in let d3 = init-all-roms 0
    in let d4 = poke-byte kbd-ring-base 0 0
    in let d5 = poke-byte kbd-ring-base 1 0
    in let boot = __record-set cpu-new "cpu-pc" (mem-read-16 #FFFC)
    in d0 + d1 + d2 + d3 + d4 + d5 + c64w-cpu-save boot

  c64w-frame : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-frame (dummy) =
    let h = __heap-save
    in let s = c64w-cpu-load 0
    in let dk = kbd-poll 0
    in let s1 = c64w-run-raster s 0
    in let dc = poke-byte cia-state 29 (bit-or (peek-byte cia-state 29) #81)
    in let s2 = cpu-irq s1
    in let ds = c64w-cpu-save s2
    in let dr = c64w-render 0
    in let restored = __heap-restore h
    in dk + dc + ds + dr

 A PC set-1 scancode into the ring `kbd-poll` drains. Byte 0 is the write
 counter, byte 1 the read counter, and the 64 slots start at 16.

  c64w-key : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-key (sc) =
    let wc = peek-byte kbd-ring-base 0
    in let d0 = poke-byte kbd-ring-base (16 + bit-and wc 63) (bit-and sc #FF)
    in d0 + poke-byte kbd-ring-base 0 (bit-and (wc + 1) #FF)

  c64w-fb-addr : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-fb-addr (dummy) = c64w-fb

  c64w-frame-width : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-frame-width (dummy) = c64w-width

  c64w-frame-height : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-frame-height (dummy) = c64w-height

 The screen and colour RAM are what a grader reads: forty by twenty-five
 screen codes is what the machine believes it is showing, independent of
 whether a single pixel was stored correctly.

  c64w-screen : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-screen (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= 1000 then (-1)
    else peek-byte c64-ram (#0400 + i)

  c64w-pc : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-pc (dummy) = peek-32 c64w-cpu 16

  c64w-halted : Integer -> Integer
  c64w-halted (dummy) = peek-32 c64w-cpu 52

